18132:
Recall the expression for the escape speed from a planet's surface:
v_e = \sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R}}
where M is the mass and R is the radius of the planet.

Express the escape speeds of planets P_1 and P_2:
Given that both planets have equal mass (M_1 = M_2 = M):
v_1 = \sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R_1}}, \quad v_2 = \sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R_2}}

Compute the ratio v_2 / v_1:
\frac{v_2}{v_1} = \sqrt{\frac{R_1}{R_2}}
Given that R_2 = \frac{R_1}{2}:
\frac{v_2}{v_1} = \sqrt{\frac{R_1}{R_1/2}} = \sqrt{2}

This matches Option (C).
→ (C)

18136:
Recall the expression for work done in an adiabatic process:
W = \frac{nR(T_1 - T_2)}{\gamma - 1}

Substitute the given values:
Number of moles: n = 1\text{ mol}
Gas constant: R = 8.3\text{ J mol}^{-1}\text{ K}^{-1}
Initial temperature: T_1 = 60\text{ K}
Final temperature: T_2 = 50\text{ K}
Adiabatic index for a monatomic gas: \gamma = \frac{5}{3}

\gamma - 1 = \frac{5}{3} - 1 = \frac{2}{3}
T_1 - T_2 = 60 - 50 = 10\text{ K}

Calculate the work done:
W = \frac{1 \times 8.3 \times 10}{2/3} = \frac{83}{2/3} = \frac{83 \times 3}{2} = \frac{249}{2} = 124.5\text{ J}

This corresponds to Option (C).
→ (C)

18145:
Calculate the least count (LC) of the Vernier caliper:
1\text{ MSD} = 1\text{ mm} = 0.1\text{ cm}.
For a standard 10-division Vernier caliper where 10\text{ VSD} = 9\text{ MSD}:
1\text{ VSD} = 0.9\text{ mm} = 0.09\text{ cm}
\text{Least Count (LC)} = 1\text{ MSD} - 1\text{ VSD} = 1\text{ mm} - 0.9\text{ mm} = 0.1\text{ mm} = 0.01\text{ cm}

Analyze the zero error:
When the jaws touch, the Vernier zero lies to the left of the main scale zero, indicating a negative zero error.
Conventionally, negative zero error is calculated as:
\text{Zero Error} = -(10 - 4) \times \text{LC} = -6 \times 0.01\text{ cm} = -0.06\text{ cm}
\text{Actual Length} = \text{Observed Length} - (\text{Zero Error}) = 1.00\text{ cm} - (-0.06\text{ cm}) = 1.06\text{ cm}
If computed simply as -4 \times \text{LC} = -0.04\text{ cm}, the actual length would be:
\text{Actual Length} = 1.00\text{ cm} - (-0.04\text{ cm}) = 1.04\text{ cm}

NTA Bonus Award:
Due to ambiguity in the definition of the zero error and the options provided in the official paper, NTA dropped this question and awarded bonus marks to all candidates.
→ (Bonus)

18148:
Apply the lens formula for convex lens L_1:
Focal length: f_1 = +10\text{ cm}
Object distance: u_1 = -30\text{ cm}
\frac{1}{v_1} - \frac{1}{u_1} = \frac{1}{f_1} \implies \frac{1}{v_1} - \frac{1}{-30} = \frac{1}{10}
\frac{1}{v_1} = \frac{1}{10} - \frac{1}{30} = \frac{3 - 1}{30} = \frac{2}{30} = \frac{1}{15} \implies v_1 = +15\text{ cm}
The first image I_1 is formed 15\text{ cm} to the right of lens L_1.

Apply the lens formula for concave lens L_2:
Lens L_2 is placed 3\text{ cm} to the right of L_1.
The image I_1 acts as a virtual object for L_2, positioned at distance:
u_2 = +15\text{ cm} - 3\text{ cm} = +12\text{ cm}
Focal length of L_2: f_2 = -10\text{ cm}
\frac{1}{v_2} - \frac{1}{u_2} = \frac{1}{f_2} \implies \frac{1}{v_2} - \frac{1}{+12} = \frac{1}{-10}
\frac{1}{v_2} = -\frac{1}{10} + \frac{1}{12} = \frac{-6 + 5}{60} = -\frac{1}{60} \implies v_2 = -60\text{ cm}

The negative sign indicates the final image is formed 60\text{ cm} to the left of the concave lens L_2.
This corresponds to Option (B).
→ (B)

18159:
Set up the centripetal force equation for the revolving electron:
In a hydrogen atom (Z = 1), the Coulomb electrostatic attraction provides the centripetal force:
\frac{m_e v^2}{r} = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\frac{e^2}{r^2}
r = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \frac{e^2}{m_e v^2}

Substitute the given values:
\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0} = 9 \times 10^9\text{ N m}^2\text{ C}^{-2}
e = 1.6 \times 10^{-19}\text{ C} \implies e^2 = 2.56 \times 10^{-38}\text{ C}^2
m_e = 9 \times 10^{-31}\text{ kg}
v = \sqrt{25.6} \times 10^5\text{ m s}^{-1} \implies v^2 = 25.6 \times 10^{10}\text{ m}^2\text{ s}^{-2}

r = \frac{(9 \times 10^9) \times (2.56 \times 10^{-38})}{(9 \times 10^{-31}) \times (25.6 \times 10^{10})}

Simplify the numerical expression:
The factor 9 cancels from numerator and denominator:
r = \frac{2.56 \times 10^{-29}}{25.6 \times 10^{-21}} = 0.1 \times 10^{-8}\text{ m} = 1 \times 10^{-9}\text{ m}

Given that r = x \times 10^{-9}\text{ m}, we obtain:
x = 1

This corresponds to Option (D).
→ (D)

18160:
Identify the condition for minimum wear and tear on tyres:
Wear and tear on tyres is minimized when the entire required centripetal force is provided solely by the horizontal component of the normal reaction, with zero sideways friction between the tyres and the road surface.
The optimum banking angle \theta is given by:
\tan\theta = \frac{v^2}{r g}

Substitute the given numerical values:
Speed: v = 10\text{ m s}^{-1}
Radius: r = 50\text{ m}
Acceleration due to gravity: g = 10\text{ m s}^{-2}

\tan\theta = \frac{10^2}{50 \times 10} = \frac{100}{500} = \frac{1}{5}
\theta = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{5}\right)

This matches Option (A).
→ (A)

18171:
Calculate the molar solubility S of each salt in water:
\text{AgBr} (AB type salt):
\text{AgBr}(s) \rightleftharpoons \text{Ag}^{+}(aq) + \text{Br}^{-}(aq) \implies K_{\text{sp}} = S^2
S = \sqrt{K_{\text{sp}}} = \sqrt{5.0 \times 10^{-13}} = \sqrt{50 \times 10^{-14}} \approx 7.07 \times 10^{-7}\text{ M}

\text{Zn(OH)}_2 (AB_2 type salt):
\text{Zn(OH)}_2(s) \rightleftharpoons \text{Zn}^{2+}(aq) + 2\text{OH}^{-}(aq) \implies K_{\text{sp}} = S(2S)^2 = 4S^3
S^3 = \frac{1.0 \times 10^{-15}}{4} = 2.5 \times 10^{-16} = 250 \times 10^{-18}
S = (250 \times 10^{-18})^{1/3} \approx 6.30 \times 10^{-6}\text{ M}

\text{Hg}_2\text{Cl}_2 (mercurous chloride, AB_2 type electrolyte forming \text{Hg}_2^{2+} and 2\text{Cl}^{-}):
\text{Hg}_2\text{Cl}_2(s) \rightleftharpoons \text{Hg}_2^{2+}(aq) + 2\text{Cl}^{-}(aq) \implies K_{\text{sp}} = 4S^3
S^3 = \frac{1.3 \times 10^{-18}}{4} = 3.25 \times 10^{-19} = 0.325 \times 10^{-18}
S = (0.325 \times 10^{-18})^{1/3} \approx 6.87 \times 10^{-7}\text{ M}

Compare the solubilities:
6.30 \times 10^{-6}\text{ M} > 7.07 \times 10^{-7}\text{ M} > 6.87 \times 10^{-7}\text{ M}
\text{Zn(OH)}_2 > \text{AgBr} > \text{Hg}_2\text{Cl}_2

This matches Option (D).
→ (D)

18176:
Write the redox reaction between oxalic acid and potassium permanganate in acidic medium:
2\text{MnO}_4^- + 5\text{C}_2\text{O}_4^{2-} + 16\text{H}^{+} \rightarrow 2\text{Mn}^{2+} + 10\text{CO}_2 + 8\text{H}_2\text{O}
In acidic medium, \text{MnO}_4^- undergoes a 5-electron reduction (n_1 = 5).
Oxalate ion (\text{C}_2\text{O}_4^{2-}) undergoes a 2-electron oxidation (n_2 = 2).

Apply the law of chemical equivalence:
\text{Equivalents of KMnO}_4 = \text{Equivalents of oxalic acid}
(M_{\text{KMnO}_4} \times n_1) \times V_1 = (M_{\text{ox}} \times n_2) \times V_2

Substitute the given values:
V_1 = 10\text{ mL}
V_2 = 10\text{ mL}
M_{\text{ox}} = 0.25\text{ M}

(M_{\text{KMnO}_4} \times 5) \times 10 = (0.25 \times 2) \times 10
5 M_{\text{KMnO}_4} = 0.50 \implies M_{\text{KMnO}_4} = \frac{0.50}{5} = 0.10\text{ M}

This matches Option (A).
→ (A)

18182:
Write the balanced chemical equation for the complete combustion of n-butane:
\text{C}_4\text{H}_{10} + \frac{13}{2}\text{O}_2 \rightarrow 4\text{CO}_2 + 5\text{H}_2\text{O}

Calculate the number of moles of butane consumed:
Molar mass of n-butane (\text{C}_4\text{H}_{10}):
M = 4(12) + 10(1) = 48 + 10 = 58\text{ g mol}^{-1}
Moles of butane in 116\text{ g}:
n_{\text{butane}} = \frac{116\text{ g}}{58\text{ g mol}^{-1}} = 2\text{ mol}

Calculate the mass of \text{CO}_2 produced:
From the stoichiometric ratio, 1\text{ mol} of \text{C}_4\text{H}_{10} yields 4\text{ mol} of \text{CO}_2:
n_{\text{CO}_2} = 2 \times 4 = 8\text{ mol}
Molar mass of \text{CO}_2 = 12 + 2(16) = 44\text{ g mol}^{-1}:
\text{Mass of } \text{CO}_2 = 8\text{ mol} \times 44\text{ g mol}^{-1} = 352\text{ g}

This corresponds to Option (A).
→ (A)

18183:
Use the linear form of the Arrhenius equation:
\ln k = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT}
From the given plot of \ln k versus 1/T:
The y-intercept (when 1/T = 0) gives:
\ln A = 6

Set up the equation for k = e^2\text{ min}^{-1}:
\ln k = \ln(e^2) = 2
Substitute \ln k = 2 and \ln A = 6:
2 = 6 - \frac{E_a}{RT} \implies \frac{E_a}{RT} = 4 \implies T = \frac{E_a}{4R}

Substitute the numerical values:
E_a = 6.64\text{ kJ mol}^{-1} = 6640\text{ J mol}^{-1}
R = 8.3\text{ J K}^{-1}\text{ mol}^{-1}
T = \frac{6640}{4 \times 8.3} = \frac{6640}{33.2} = 200\text{ K}

This matches Option (C).
→ (C)

18185:
Determine the electronic configuration and number of unpaired electrons (n) for each complex:
(A) [\text{Mn(CN)}_6]^{3-}:
\text{Mn}^{3+} (3d^4). \text{CN}^{-} is a strong field ligand, causing pairing in t_{2g}:
t_{2g}^4 e_g^0 \implies n = 2 \text{ unpaired electrons}
\mu = \sqrt{2(2 + 2)} = \sqrt{8} \approx 2.83\text{ BM}
