18193:
Analyze the equilibrium between native (N) and denatured (D) protein:
\text{N} \rightleftharpoons \text{D}
At 60^\circ\text{C}, the concentrations of native and denatured states are equal at equilibrium:
[\text{N}] = [\text{D}]
The equilibrium constant is:
K = \frac{[\text{D}]}{[\text{N}]} = 1

Relate standard Gibbs free energy to equilibrium constant:
\Delta G^\circ = -RT \ln K = -RT \ln(1) = 0

Calculate the standard entropy change (\Delta S^\circ):
\Delta G^\circ = \Delta H^\circ - T\Delta S^\circ = 0 \implies \Delta S^\circ = \frac{\Delta H^\circ}{T}
Given:
\Delta H^\circ = 666\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}
T = 60^\circ\text{C} = 60 + 273.15 = 333.15\text{ K} \approx 333\text{ K}

\Delta S^\circ = \frac{666\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}}{333\text{ K}} = 2.0\text{ kJ K}^{-1}\text{ mol}^{-1}

This matches Option (A).
→ (A)

18252:
In arithmetic growth (NCERT Biology - Plant Growth and Development):
The length of an organ at time t is expressed by the linear equation:
L_t = L_0 + r \cdot t

Given:
Initial length at time t = 0: L_0 = 20\text{ cm}
Arithmetic growth rate: r = 30\text{ cm/day}
Time elapsed: t = 7\text{ days}

Substituting these values:
L_7 = 20 + 30 \times 7 = 20 + 210 = 230\text{ cm}

Thus, the length of the stem at the end of the 7th day is 230\text{ cm}.
